    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Caulking wood siding is a different problem from caulking anything else, because the substrate will not hold still. A cedar board can move a millimetre across its width between August and February, every year, and a sealant that cannot accommodate that will split — usually down the middle of the bead, usually in year two. Elongation, not weather resistance, is the specification that matters.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-wood-differs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Wood Siding Is a Different Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most exterior caulk is formulated with masonry, fibre cement and vinyl in mind. Those materials are dimensionally stable — they expand and contract with temperature, modestly and predictably. Wood does something else entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timber takes on and gives up moisture with the humidity around it, and it moves as it does. That movement is mostly across the grain rather than along it, and it can be substantial: a nominal six-inch cedar board can change width by a millimetre or more between a humid summer and a dry winter, every single year, for the life of the building.
          </p>

          <p>
            A caulk joint between that board and a fixed trim piece therefore opens and closes seasonally. A sealant that cures hard, or that stretches but does not recover, cannot survive that cycle indefinitely. It splits down the centre of the bead or peels away from one face, and either way water gets in behind the siding — which is worse than no caulk at all, because now it cannot dry out.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the whole reason this is a separate question from general exterior caulking. Our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">general exterior caulk guide</a> covers the broader range across all substrates; this one is specifically about the joints where wood movement is the governing constraint.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Specification That Decides It</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Elongation and recovery, not weather resistance.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every exterior caulk claims weatherproofing. Very few state how far they stretch and return. On wood, look for 100 percent joint movement as a minimum and 300 percent or more for wide or exposed trim joints — that number predicts survival better than anything else on the tube.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="elongation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Elongation: Reading the Number That Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Elongation is how far a cured bead can stretch before it tears, expressed as a percentage of its original dimension. Recovery is whether it returns when the joint closes again. Both matter, and only the first is usually printed.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Elongation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Suits</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">On Wood</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
