          <p>
            The top row is the one that causes most of the failures people ask about. Ordinary painter's caulk is an interior product that happens to be sold in the exterior aisle, and it is what a great many houses are caulked with because it is cheap and tools beautifully. On wood siding it lasts a season or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bottom row explains why pure silicone, despite excellent movement figures, is the wrong choice on trim you intend to paint: nothing sticks to it. Paintable siliconised and hybrid products exist precisely to resolve that conflict, and they are what belong on painted wood.
          </p>
