        <div id="where-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Siding Joints You Should Not Caulk
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part that saves houses, and it runs against instinct. Not every gap in siding is a defect — some are drainage and ventilation paths, and sealing them traps water inside the wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Bottom Edge of Lap Siding</h3>

          <p>
            The horizontal gap where each board laps the one below is designed to let any water that gets behind the siding drain out and the assembly dry. Caulking that line seals water in. This is the single most common and most damaging caulking mistake on a wood-sided house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weep Holes and Drainage Gaps</h3>

          <p>
            Anywhere the wall assembly is designed to breathe or drain — weep holes above windows, gaps at the bottom of a rainscreen, the underside of a window sill. Filling them converts a wall that manages water into one that stores it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Butt Joints Between Siding Boards, in Some Systems</h3>

          <p>
            Whether these should be caulked depends on the siding system and the manufacturer's instructions. Many modern systems use flashing behind the joint and specifically instruct that it be left uncaulked. Check the installation guide rather than assuming.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Should Be Caulked</h3>

          <p>
            Vertical joints where siding meets trim, window and door casings, corner boards, and penetrations for pipes, vents and fittings. These are the places water enters and where there is no drainage path behind. Our <a href="/where-not-to-caulk/">guide to where not to caulk</a> covers the full list across the building.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="joint-design" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Joint Design and Backer Rod
          </h2>

          <p>
            How a bead is shaped decides how much movement it can take, and it is the part of the job nobody is taught.
          </p>

          <p>
            A sealant bead should adhere to two faces, not three. If it also bonds to the back of the joint, it cannot stretch — it is anchored in the middle and tears when the joint opens. This is called three-sided adhesion and it is why beads in deep joints fail even when the product was right.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is backer rod: a foam cylinder pushed into the joint before caulking, which both fills the depth and gives the bead a non-adhering back face. It is inexpensive, it reduces how much caulk you use, and on any joint deeper than about a quarter of an inch it roughly doubles the useful life of the seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The target profile is a bead about twice as wide as it is deep, with an hourglass cross-section — thicker at the edges where it bonds, thinner in the middle where it stretches. Tooling with a wet finger or a caulk tool creates that shape; running a fat bead and leaving it does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            For joints wider than about half an inch, caulk is the wrong answer entirely. That is a trim or flashing problem, and filling it with sealant produces a bead that will fail regardless of product — no sealant is a structural filler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="painting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Painting Over Caulk
          </h2>

          <p>
            On wood trim, almost every bead gets painted, and the interaction between the two is where a good caulk job becomes a visible one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Respect the paint-ready time.</strong> Latex products are usually paintable within 30 minutes to an hour; elastomerics and hybrids often want considerably longer, sometimes a full day. Painting too early traps solvent or water in the bead, and the paint film cracks as it escapes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expect movement to show at the paint line.</strong> Paint is far less flexible than an elastomeric sealant. A bead that stretches 300 percent will crack its paint film long before it fails itself, which looks like caulk failure and is not. On high-movement joints this is unavoidable, and it is an argument for a colour-matched sealant left unpainted where the aesthetics allow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Prime bare wood first, then caulk.</strong> Caulking before priming lets the bare timber absorb moisture from the sealant and gives poorer adhesion. Prime, caulk, then topcoat — that order produces the longest-lasting joint and it is what every manufacturer specifies.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not caulk over paint that is failing.</strong> The bead bonds to the old paint, and when that paint lets go the caulk goes with it. Scrape back to sound material first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Application and Weather
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior caulking is weather-dependent in ways people routinely underestimate, and most premature failures trace back to conditions rather than product.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Condition</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Latex / Elastomeric</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hybrid Polymer</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Minimum temperature</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40°F and rising</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Near freezing, some below</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Damp surfaces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No — must be dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Many tolerate damp</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rain after application</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hours clear needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 2-4 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Direct hot sun</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Skins too fast; work shaded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">More tolerant</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ideal window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-80°F, overcast, dry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The temperature row is the one that catches people out every autumn. Latex-based sealants applied below about 40°F never develop proper adhesion, even though they appear to gun and tool normally. The bead looks perfect in October and is peeling away by March, and the product gets blamed for a conditions problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other common error is caulking in mid-summer at the peak of the wood's expansion. A joint caulked when it is at its narrowest has to stretch the furthest come winter. Spring and autumn, when the timber is mid-range, give the bead the easiest life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle small and make several passes rather than one large bead. A thick bead looks like thorough work and is actually worse — it has more material to shrink, tools poorly, and is more likely to achieve three-sided adhesion in a deep joint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Long a Bead Should Last
          </h2>

          <p>
            Realistic expectations help, because caulk is maintenance rather than a permanent repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            On wood siding, a good elastomeric bead in a properly designed joint should give you eight to fifteen years. A hybrid polymer in the same joint often reaches fifteen or twenty. Ordinary acrylic latex on the same wall gives one to three, which is why so many houses seem to need recaulking constantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect annually, ideally in late winter when the joints are at their widest and any failure is most visible. What you are looking for is splitting down the centre of the bead, peeling away from one face, or a bead that has gone hard and chalky. Any of those means water can get in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repair by removal rather than by adding. New caulk over old bonds to the old bead, not to the wood, and inherits its failure. Cut the old bead out with a utility knife and a caulk removal tool, clean the joint faces, and start again — it takes longer and it is the only approach that lasts.
          </p>

          <p>
            And keep the paint film maintained, because on painted trim the paint is the first line of defence and the caulk is the second. A wall whose paint is sound rarely stresses its caulk joints; one that has been left to weather puts everything on the sealant. Our <a href="/why-does-exterior-caulk-keep-cracking/">guide to cracking caulk</a> works through the diagnosis when beads fail repeatedly in the same place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Painter's Caulk Outside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary acrylic latex handles 10 to 25 percent movement and wood siding demands far more. It tools beautifully and costs very little, which is why it is on so many houses — and why those houses need recaulking every two or three years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking the Bottom Edge of Lap Siding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                That gap is a drainage and ventilation path, not a defect. Sealing it traps any water that gets behind the siding inside the wall assembly, which is considerably worse than leaving it open. It is the most damaging caulking mistake on a wood-sided house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Backer Rod in Deep Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bead bonded to the back of the joint as well as the two faces cannot stretch — it is anchored in the middle and tears when the joint opens. Backer rod costs very little, uses less caulk, and roughly doubles the life of the seal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking in Cold Weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Latex-based sealants applied below about 40°F never develop proper adhesion, even though they gun and tool normally at the time. The bead looks perfect in autumn and is peeling by spring. Use a hybrid polymer if the work cannot wait for warmer weather.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking Over an Old Failing Bead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New caulk bonds to the old bead rather than the wood, and inherits whatever made the old one fail. Cut the old material out with a knife and a removal tool, clean both joint faces, and start from bare material.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk for wood siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sashco Big Stretch is our overall pick, because its 500 percent elongation accommodates the seasonal movement that splits ordinary caulk at wood trim joints. DAP Dynaflex 230 is the better value for a whole house, and OSI QUAD MAX is the choice where the bead stays unpainted or the weather is cold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does caulk keep cracking on my wood trim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always because the product cannot handle the movement. Wood swells and shrinks seasonally, and acrylic latex caulk tolerates only 10 to 25 percent movement. Use an elastomeric rated for at least 100 percent, and fit backer rod in deep joints so the bead can stretch instead of being anchored at the back.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I caulk the bottom edge of lap siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. That gap lets water which gets behind the siding drain out and the wall dry. Caulking it seals moisture inside the assembly and causes rot. Caulk the vertical joints where siding meets trim, window and door casings, corner boards and penetrations — not the horizontal laps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What elongation do I need for wood siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least 100 percent joint movement as a minimum, and 300 percent or more for wide or exposed trim joints. Ordinary painter's caulk manages 10 to 25 percent, which is why it fails within a season or two. The figure is on the technical data sheet even when it is not on the tube.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint over exterior caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over paintable products, yes — but respect the paint-ready time, which can be a full day for elastomerics rather than the 30 minutes latex needs. Prime bare wood first, then caulk, then topcoat. Pure silicone cannot be painted at all, which is why paintable siliconised and hybrid products exist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need backer rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In any joint deeper than about a quarter of an inch, yes. It prevents the bead bonding to the back of the joint, which would anchor it in the middle and make it tear when the joint opens. It also uses less caulk and helps you achieve the hourglass profile that stretches best.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should exterior caulk last on wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Eight to fifteen years for a good elastomeric in a well-designed joint, and fifteen to twenty for a hybrid polymer. Ordinary acrylic latex gives one to three. Inspect in late winter when joints are widest, and repair by cutting the old bead out rather than caulking over it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

