          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On wood, <strong class="text-white">elongation is the specification that matters</strong>. <strong class="text-white">Sashco Big Stretch</strong> for the joints that move most, <strong class="text-white">DAP Dynaflex 230</strong> for the other ninety percent of the house, and <strong class="text-white">OSI QUAD MAX</strong> where the bead stays unpainted or the weather turns cold.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then get the joint right: <strong class="text-white">backer rod in anything deep, two faces of adhesion not three, and never caulk the bottom edge of lap siding</strong>. A cheap sealant in a well-designed joint outlasts a premium one in a badly designed one.
